    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Что ты здесь делаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What are you doing here?' Notice how 'what' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'are you doing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Что не на работе?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why aren't you at work?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'what' with 'work'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Опять допрос.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Another interrogation.' Notice how 'again' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'interrogation'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы сговорились что ли?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you plan this or something?' Kostya combines 'you' with 'really' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Я сегодня во вторую смену.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am on the second shift today.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'shift'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отлично. Я с тобой поеду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Great. I will go with you.' Kostya links 'perfect' directly with 'I' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Может ты забыл,",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe you forgot,' Notice how 'can' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'forgot'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, я помню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yeah, I remember.' Kostya combines 'yes' with 'remember' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Меня уволили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I got fired.' Here the verb for 'to fire' takes the plural past-tense ending '-li', which is also used for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "А что ты не у Вики остался?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why didn't you stay at Vika's?' Max uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'remained', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Она меня не пустила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She wouldn't let me in.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'to let go' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она все усложнять начала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She started making everything complicated.' Kostya combines 'it' with 'beginning' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
